import{_ as t,o as a,c as s,ao as r}from"./chunks/framework.BQjsKH0C.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Performance","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"performance.md","filePath":"performance.md","lastUpdated":null}'),n={name:"performance.md"};function i(o,e,d,l,h,c){return a(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r(`<h1 id="Performance" tabindex="-1">Performance <a class="header-anchor" href="#Performance" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Performance {#Performance}&quot;">​</a></h1><h2 id="The-shared-CPU-engine-and-its-threads" tabindex="-1">The shared CPU engine and its threads <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-shared-CPU-engine-and-its-threads" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The shared CPU engine and its threads {#The-shared-CPU-engine-and-its-threads}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>All tenferro-rs operations of a process run on one shared CPU execution engine. The engine keeps its buffer pool, which recycles output allocations, and its kernel plan cache between calls and across contexts. Calls from different contexts and Julia threads take turns on it.</p><p>By default the engine is serial. To run kernels on a worker pool, set the <code>TENFERRO_NUM_THREADS</code> environment variable before the first tensor operation, or call <code>Tenferro.set_num_threads</code> at any time:</p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">using</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> Tenferro</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">Tenferro</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">set_num_threads</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">4</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)   </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># rebuilds the engine with 4 workers</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">Tenferro</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">num_threads</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">()        </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># 4</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Changing the thread count rebuilds the engine, so its buffer pool and plan cache start empty. Tensors and contexts created before the change remain valid. Threaded kernels may sum in a different order than serial ones, so results can differ in the last bits. The default stays serial so that results are reproducible and the engine does not compete with Julia or BLAS threads.</p><h2 id="Reusing-derivative-programs" tabindex="-1">Reusing derivative programs <a class="header-anchor" href="#Reusing-derivative-programs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Reusing derivative programs {#Reusing-derivative-programs}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>All eager contexts share one tenferro-rs eager runtime. Its caches of compiled derivative programs and AD transforms persist across contexts and DifferentiationInterface calls, and tenferro-ad bounds them at 128 entries and 64 MiB each. The cache key is the structure of the recorded computation and the shapes and element types of its inputs, not their values. A second <code>gradient</code> call on the same function with new data of the same shapes therefore reuses the compiled reverse pass, even when the function is a new closure.</p><p>Each DifferentiationInterface call closes its context before it returns, which frees the tenferro-rs graph at once. Contexts left for Julia&#39;s garbage collector would stay registered in the shared runtime, and every reverse pass scans the tensors of all live contexts, so steps would get slower until a collection runs.</p><p>Gradients, and each seed of a pullback, are computed for all inputs in one reverse pass. On the traced backend, the value and all gradients are compiled and run as one program. A <code>TracedContext</code> keeps the value of every node it has evaluated until it is closed, so reading a value again does not recompile.</p><p>Arrays and numbers that are mixed into tensor operations, <code>Tenferro.constant</code> values and <code>DifferentiationInterface.Constant</code> data are untracked constants. No gradient is computed for them, and they do not take part in reverse passes.</p><h2 id="Measurements" tabindex="-1">Measurements <a class="header-anchor" href="#Measurements" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Measurements {#Measurements}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>These are medians on an Apple M-series machine with 16 cores, Julia 1.13 and Float32, with the best time in parentheses. The MNIST steps are one <code>TenferroLux.withgradient</code> call, which is one <code>DifferentiationInterface.value_and_gradient</code> call. The MLP is 784→128→64→10 with batch 128. The CNN is Conv(5×5, 1→8) → MaxPool → Conv(3×3, 8→16) → MaxPool → Dense(400→10) with batch 128. Losses and gradients are bitwise identical in every column.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">Workload</th><th style="text-align:right;">before (44aac49)</th><th style="text-align:right;">shared engine</th><th style="text-align:right;">+ derivative reuse</th><th style="text-align:right;">+ 4 threads</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;">tiny eager op, <code>exp.(x)</code> with 4 elements</td><td style="text-align:right;">16.3 µs</td><td style="text-align:right;">12.8 µs</td><td style="text-align:right;">12.6 µs</td><td style="text-align:right;">15.5 µs</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">eager <code>reshape</code> (adds a graph node)</td><td style="text-align:right;">15.2 µs</td><td style="text-align:right;">12.4 µs</td><td style="text-align:right;">12.0 µs</td><td style="text-align:right;">12.7 µs</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">eager matmul, 128×784 · 784×128</td><td style="text-align:right;">342 µs</td><td style="text-align:right;">321 µs</td><td style="text-align:right;">316 µs</td><td style="text-align:right;">189 µs</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">MLP step, <code>AutoTenferro</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">24.9 (20.6) ms</td><td style="text-align:right;">21.9 (19.6) ms</td><td style="text-align:right;">3.3 (2.8) ms</td><td style="text-align:right;">3.4 (2.7) ms</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">MLP step, <code>AutoTenferroTraced</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">23.1 (18.2) ms</td><td style="text-align:right;">18.5 (16.6) ms</td><td style="text-align:right;">4.9 (4.2) ms</td><td style="text-align:right;">4.4 (4.1) ms</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">CNN step, <code>AutoTenferro</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">246 (224) ms</td><td style="text-align:right;">201 (189) ms</td><td style="text-align:right;">71 (69) ms</td><td style="text-align:right;">47 (45) ms</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">CNN step, <code>AutoTenferroTraced</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">273 (234) ms</td><td style="text-align:right;">193 (188) ms</td><td style="text-align:right;">71 (67) ms</td><td style="text-align:right;">41 (38) ms</td></tr></tbody></table><p>For reference, Zygote takes 0.65 ms for the MLP step and 30 ms for the CNN step on the same machine. With 8 threads, the CNN steps were slower than with 4 threads. Small models such as the MLP are dominated by per-call overhead rather than kernel time and gain nothing from threads.</p><h2 id="Where-the-time-goes" tabindex="-1">Where the time goes <a class="header-anchor" href="#Where-the-time-goes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where the time goes {#Where-the-time-goes}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each Tenferro operation crosses into Rust. A call that only adds a graph node costs about 11–12 µs, almost all of it on the Rust side: semantic recording and runtime bookkeeping. The RustCall boundary itself costs about 1 µs. Models with many small operations are therefore limited by the number of operations, not by kernel time.</p>`,17)])])}const m=t(n,[["render",i]]);export{p as __pageData,m as default};
